<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="f1bcbc2c" lines="40-55">
<description>
Paint the top-connected middle channel between two orange path components teal.

Input structure
- The background is black.
- One foreground color forms exactly two 4-connected one-pixel-wide path components.
- Optional non-background control pixels may sit in the channel between the two paths.

Rule
- Dilate each path component by one cell in the 8-neighborhood and intersect the two dilations; this gives the one-cell-wide middle channel.
- A control pixel in that channel is a blocker only when its 4-neighbors touch fewer than both path components; otherwise it is part of the channel and is repainted.
- After removing blockers, paint the channel component connected to the top edge teal and keep all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The desired pixels are not found by region filling; they are the geometric overlap of the two paths' one-cell halos, with weakly attached control pixels splitting the channel.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="ba1aa698" lines="41-55">
<description>
Extrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.

Input structure
- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.
- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.
- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.

Rule
- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.
- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.
- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.

Key insight
- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="753ea09b" lines="60-79">
<description>
Fill the small background compartments bounded by the long contour color.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and two foreground colors.
- The more frequent foreground color forms long 8-connected contour fragments.
- The other foreground color is a fixed distractor/obstacle color that partitions
  the background but is never recolored.

Rule:
- Split the background into 4-connected regions using all foreground pixels as
  barriers.
- Keep the two largest background regions as the outside of the contour drawing.
- Recolor every other background region that touches a long contour component
  to the contour color; leave all foreground pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
The apparent area fill is exactly a component-level fill of bounded background
compartments, not a row-wise interpolation between individual contour pixels.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="ad3b40cf" lines="72-87">
<description>
Mirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.
- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.

Rule
- Preserve the input grid.
- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.
- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.

Key insight
- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="db0c5428" lines="32-51">
<description>
Expand a 3x3 block motif into a 5x5 block motif around its missing center.

Input structure:
- Teal background surrounds one 9x9 foreground motif.
- The motif is a 3x3 array of 3x3 blocks; the middle block is empty teal.
- Colored pixels immediately around the empty middle block encode the border
  of the missing center block.

Rule:
- Keep the original 9x9 motif in place.
- Copy each of the eight surrounding 3x3 blocks to the opposite outer slot
  of the implied 5x5 block grid, without rotating it.
- Fill the center block from the eight pixels around the hole; its center is
  the motif's majority foreground color.

Key insight: The visible 9x9 motif is the middle of a larger periodic
block-level pattern, and the empty center is reconstructed from its immediate
inner boundary.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="332202d5" lines="49-64">
<description>
Fill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.

Input structure
- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.
- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.
- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.

Rule
- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.
- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.
- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.

Key insight
- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

